    <string name="traditions_sources_description">Where each tradition comes from, and how Auspice computes it</string>
    <string name="traditions_sources_text">Everything is calculated on your device, from the date and the name. Traditions are shown as cultural trivia, never as advice or prediction.\n\n<b>Western astrology</b>: tropical sun signs with the conventional cusp dates, which can be one day off for people born on a cusp.\n\n<b>Chinese astrology</b>: the sexagenary cycle of animals, elements and yin/yang, using the real lunar new year from the Chinese calendar.\n\n<b>Numerology</b>: Pythagorean system. Master numbers 11, 22 and 33 are kept. Name numbers use the name and surname as entered.\n\n<b>Mayan calendar</b>: the 260-day Tzolk\'in count with the standard GMT correlation.\n\n<b>Birthstones and flowers</b>: the modern birthstone list and traditional birth flowers.\n\n<b>Weekday lore</b>: the English \"Monday\'s child\" rhyme, Thai lucky colours and Akan day names from Ghana.\n\n<b>Islamic calendar</b>: the Umm al-Qura Hijri date provided by the system.\n\n<b>Hebrew calendar</b>: the arithmetic Hebrew calendar, checked day by day against independent implementations. A Hebrew day starts at sunset, so someone born in the evening may have a Hebrew birthday one day later than shown. People born in Adar celebrate in Adar II in leap years.\n\n<b>Celtic tree calendar</b>: thirteen tree months linked to the Ogham alphabet. It is a modern construction, popularised by Robert Graves in 1948, not an ancient Celtic calendar.\n\n<b>This birthday</b>: golden birthdays, Ben Ming Nian, Japanese longevity ages (kanreki, kiju, beiju and more), Korean hwangap, South Indian shashtipoorthi, numerology cycles and lucky or unlucky numbers. Yakudoshi differs for men and women, so it only appears when the optional sex field is set on a birthday.\n\n<b>Numerology systems</b>: Pythagorean assigns 1 to 9 in alphabet order. Chaldean assigns 1 to 8 by sound and also shows the unreduced \"compound\" number.\n\n<b>Moon, rising and Vedic signs</b>: computed from the positions of the Sun and Moon (Meeus, Astronomical Algorithms) when the birth year is known. Without a birth time, every sign the Moon passed through that day is listed. The rising sign needs both the birth time and the birth place. Vedic signs use the sidereal zodiac with the Lahiri ayanamsa.\n\n<b>Personality type</b>: entered by hand or found with an original self-reflection test in the style of Myers-Briggs types. It is not the official MBTI® assessment.\n\n<b>Compatibility</b>: Western element harmony, the Chinese harmony trines, secret friends and clashes, and numerology Life Path families.</string>
